// games/sonic4/test/poison/poison_band_base_above.emp — POISON (see README).
//
// TARGETS: OWN-1's base rule — a band opening on an entry that no longer holds this
//          frame's base-DMA value (band-ownership design §3.2 / §3.3's last row).
// EXPECTED FRAGMENT: "does not hold this frame's base palette"
//
// An unbanded tint at line 50 writes CRAM entry 36, and a band at 100..140 then opens over
// the same entry. Everything about the band is well-formed; the fault is entirely in what
// was there before it.
//
// A `pal_restore` streams this frame's BASE palette back, not "this band's contribution
// removed". So from line 140 down, entry 36 reverts to BASE — not to the line-50 colour
// the author put there. The line-50 effect silently stops at 140.
//
// THIS IS THE ROW WHERE THE NEW RULE EARNS ITS KEEP AS DOCUMENTATION. C-A refused this
// program too, by counting two strictly-earlier ops intersecting the restore's span and
// reporting "2 strictly-earlier op(s) intersect the restore's span" — a sentence about the
// guard's own bookkeeping, from which the author had to reconstruct what would go wrong.
// The walk can say the true thing instead, naming the entry, the line and the mechanism.
// Keep that property in mind before rewording the guard: the fragment this case matches is
// the half of the sentence that describes the PROGRAM.
//
// ONE WORD, so the walk reports one sentence rather than one per covered entry.
//
// COUNT 1.
//
// NO IMPORTS — see poison_two_restores.emp's note.
module games.sonic4.test.poison.poison_band_base_above

const POISON_BAND_BASE_ABOVE = compose([
    [ fire(50, [stream_cram($48, [$0EEE])]) ],
    band(100, 140, stream_cram($48, [$0AAA]), 0),
])

ensure(raster_program(POISON_BAND_BASE_ABOVE).len > 0,
       "poison_band_base_above: raster_program returned nothing — this poison no longer reaches the guard it targets")
